Write Shen byte strings as bytes (shen-scheme v0.52). PENDING UPSTREAM:
the same change, with tests, is branch upstream/byte-strings of
pyrex41/shen-scheme, on top of tizoc/shen-scheme fa07173. It has not been
proposed upstream yet.

The kernel's reader makes one character of each byte of a source file, and
the kernel's own `pr` writes each character back as one byte. shen-scheme's
faster `pr` override instead writes `(string->utf8 String)`, so every byte
above 127 is UTF-8-encoded a second time: "λ雪 café" (11 bytes) prints as 18.
This patch:
- writes a character below 256 as that byte, and one at or above 256 (made
  only on the Scheme side) as UTF-8;
- passes file names to the OS as the UTF-8 their bytes hold, so `load` of a
  non-ASCII path works.

The release tarball ships the sources precompiled, so the one generated line
in compiled/overrides.scm is patched too. Precompiling the patched
src/overrides.shen gives that same line; the rest of the tree differs only by
gensym numbering.

